 * Parameterised query functions for the /users resource.
 *
 * Security guarantees
 * ───────────────────
 * • Every external value is passed as a positional parameter ($1, $2, …) via
 *   pg's parameterised-query interface. The pg driver sends the query text and
 *   the parameter list to PostgreSQL in *separate protocol messages*, so the
 *   database server never interpolates user input into SQL text. This is the
 *   canonical defence against SQL injection and is enforced at the protocol
 *   level, not just by string escaping.
 *
 * • Integer parameters (id, limit, offset) are coerced and range-checked in
 *   JavaScript *before* being forwarded to the database. This prevents
 *   malformed inputs (e.g. "1; DROP TABLE users") from reaching the query at all.
 *
 * • The search query string is validated for maximum length to prevent
 *   resource-exhaustion via enormous ILIKE patterns.
 *
 * • No raw string concatenation is used anywhere in this file.
 *
 * Performance / observability
 * ───────────────────────────
 * • Every query is wrapped in queryWithTiming(), which records wall-clock
 *   duration and logs it with contextual metadata.
 *
 * • Slow queries (> SLOW_QUERY_THRESHOLD_MS) are logged at WARN level so they
 *   can be spotted without enabling full query logs in production.
 *
 * • Pool connections are acquired and released automatically via pool.query().
 *   Explicit client checkout (pool.connect()) is only used when a transaction
 *   spans multiple statements.
